{
  "id": "COMPOUND:31160 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Phytate is implicated in the interaction with DNA polymerase iota through a series of molecular pathways, where it acts as a target for multiple compounds. The evidence suggests that Phytate serves as a common target for three distinct compounds—3Alpha,16Alpha-Dihydroxylanosta-7,9(11),24-Trien-21-Oic Acid, 1,3-Dibromo-2-propanone, and 2-Oxoagelasine B—all of which are known to target DNA polymerase iota. This indicates a potential mechanism where Phytate may act as a substrate or modulator for these compounds, influencing their ability to interact with DNA polymerase iota. The shared target relationship implies that Phytate may play a role in the regulation or inhibition of DNA polymerase iota, possibly through a common pathway or by facilitating the binding of these compounds. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Phytate is a key player in the interaction network involving DNA polymerase iota, though the exact mechanism of its involvement remains to be fully elucidated.",
